WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:11.530
Hello and welcome to the first lecture of
the Bioinformatics Algorithms class!

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:16.590
When I started teaching bioinformatics two
decades ago, one of the biggest challenges

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:23.590
was to connect with a very diverse audience.
I had to talk to computer scientists and biologists,

00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:31.999
to physicists and biochemists, to mathematicians
and statisticians.

00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:38.999
The challenge was how to keep computer scientists
focused while explaining sometimes convoluted

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:44.769
biological concepts.
And likewise, how to keep biologists awake

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:48.890
while explaining sometimes complex algorithmic
ideas.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:55.339
I hope that in this class we have an equally
diverse audience, and that's why I decided

00:00:55.339 --> 00:01:02.140
to start from a very simple problem.
Here is the first problem in this class.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:09.140
Given a string called Text1, copy it to a
string called Text2.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.780
And I see that some of you are already thinking
about quitting this this class because it

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.970
is so trivial.
Please wait. You might think that it is trivial,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:27.970
but billions of cells (replicating your own
genome right now) don't think so.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:35.330
In fact, they are trying to accomplish the
incredibly complex task of replicating genome.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.460
Just think about this: you need to unwind
DNA (two strands of DNA) and separate them.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.910
You need to make sure that there are enough
free-floating nucleotides so replication can

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:52.890
complete because if you start replication
and you have to stop in the middle because

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:59.070
there is no energy left, you are dead.
So, in this course, we will learn about this

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:06.060
and many other challenges related to replication.
In fact, replication of the human genome is

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:12.480
so complex that biologists still do not fully
understand how its done.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.860
That's why we will focus on replication of
bacterial genomes that are 1000 times smaller

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:24.820
(their genomes are 1000 times smaller) and
that's why replicating bacterial genomes is

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.860
a little bit easier. I am not saying that
it is trivial.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:36.860
And in fact, right now, there are billions
of bacterial cells trying to replicate bacterial

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:43.950
genomes inside you because the number of bacterial
cells inside you is 10 times larger than the

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:51.069
number of your own human cells.
Now, since bacteria usually have circular

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.200
chromosomes, I have to slightly change my
problem.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.060
My new problem is:
Given a CIRCULAR genome called Text1, copy

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:09.060
it to a CIRCULAR Text2.
It is equally trivial, but it makes some slight

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.470
changes in the setup that we need to address.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:19.470
A circular genome does not have a start and
an end, and that's why the roughly 5 million

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:25.020
nucleotide-long E. coli genome has a choice
on where to start replication.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:32.020
In this class, we will try to figure out where
it starts because all E. coli cells on Earth

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.190
start replication in the same region, called
the "origin of replication".

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:46.099
So, our first problem is "Where in the genome
does it all begin?" and the only information

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:53.099
that we have is the genome itself.
Let's go ahead and first try to answer the

00:03:55.110 --> 00:04:00.040
question: What are the hidden messages in
the replication origin that help the E. coli

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.150
genome figure out where to start.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:10.150
And here is the first, or should I say the
3rd problem in this course: finding the origin

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:15.620
of replication. I'll give you a genome, you
will have to give me the location of origin

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:21.459
of replication in this genome.
Does the problem make sense?

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:25.930
Yes [from the audience]
You may be right or wrong depending on who

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.159
you are. "Are you a computer scientist or
a biologist?"

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:34.559
Computer scientist [from the audience]
Then you are wrong. If you were a biologist

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:39.629
then you could think about an experiment.
For example, you can start cutting short pieces

00:04:39.629 --> 00:04:46.629
from DNA, and when you cut a piece and the
cell suddenly lost ability to replicate, it

00:04:48.029 --> 00:04:52.469
means that probably you cut out the origin
of replication.

00:04:52.469 --> 00:04:59.029
But if you are a computer scientist, you should
just refuse solving this problem; it is an

00:04:59.029 --> 00:05:05.909
ill-defined computational problem.
So, how can we turn this ill-defined computational

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:12.249
problem into something that makes sense?
Lets try to ask a question: "how does the

00:05:12.249 --> 00:05:17.249
cell know to begin replication in short oriC
region?"

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:24.249
There must be some hidden messages in the
genome that tell the cell "Start replication

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:33.409
right here!"
What are these hidden messages?

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:40.409
Let's first try to formulate the problem right.
The right formulation will be the Hidden Message

00:05:40.529 --> 00:05:45.349
Problem.
In this case, Input is a string Text representing

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:51.050
the replication origin, and Output is the
hidden message in Text.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:55.469
Is this problem clear?
If you feel this problem clear, please raise

00:05:55.469 --> 00:06:01.969
your hands.
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8...

00:06:01.969 --> 00:06:05.419
This problem is absolutely unclear.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:12.419
It is equally poorly defined as the first
problem I showed you. Indeed, Son is not happy

00:06:12.619 --> 00:06:18.770
again because what is a hidden message?
Have I described it? No.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:25.529
So, for a computer scientist there is not
even a starting point to address this problem.

00:06:25.529 --> 00:06:31.770
But let's go two centuries back and let's
see how somebody in a somewhat similar situation

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:38.770
tried to solve a similar problem.
We go to "The Gold Bug" short story by Edgar

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:46.509
Allan Poe; in this story, the hero, whose
name was Legrand, tried to decode the message

00:06:46.619 --> 00:06:51.119
left by pirates.
And his hope was that when he decoded the

00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:56.550
message, he would be able to find pirate treasure,
so he was quite motivated.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:02.059
The only thing he saw is this text.
How would you tell what is written, what is

00:07:02.059 --> 00:07:05.800
coded in this text?
What Legrand noticed is that a combination

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:12.800
of three symbols ";48" appears surprisingly
frequently in this text.

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:18.379
Does it suggest an idea on what is written
in the message?

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:25.379
Here is the hint: The message is in English"
And here is another hint: "THE" is the most

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:32.909
frequent word in English
And that's why he was able to substitute ";48"

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:40.219
for "THE" and this message started to make
sense. And if you have time, you can complete

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:47.430
the decoding.
Now, if our original Hidden Message Problem

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:56.589
was clearly ill-defined, let's now try to
redefine it so that it would make sense even

00:07:56.589 --> 00:08:01.939
for a computer scientist.
How we would redefine it? Well, for various

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:08.939
biological signals, certain words appear surprisingly
frequently in the text. For example, on this

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:18.919
slide, AATTT appears surprisingly frequently
in a short text -- its unlikely that it would

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:20.979
happen by chance.

00:08:20.979 --> 00:08:27.779
Then we formulate our first real Rosalind
problem. The Frequent Words Problem is: "I'll

00:08:27.779 --> 00:08:34.779
give you a string and an integer k, and you
need to find all most frequent k-mers in Text,

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:40.259
which means all most frequent strings of length
k in the Text.

00:08:40.259 --> 00:08:47.259
Is the problem clear now? Let's ask Son.
He feels better, but he is still not clear

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:53.519
because he does not know what exactly a "most
frequent word" means.

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:59.459
I think he is a little bit picky today because
it's easy to define what a most frequent word

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:04.209
means.
A k-mer Pattern (a string of length k) is

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:11.209
a most frequent word in Text if no other k-mer
is more frequent than Pattern.

00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:18.490
And now even Son is satisfied and I want to
thank Son for great help with development

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:25.490
of this lecture.
Now, Son is satisfied but we should ask biologists

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:32.930
whether this problem actually makes sense.
Remember, bioinformatics is about connecting

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:38.610
computational and experimental ideas.
And if we check with biologists, it also looks

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:45.610
that they are quite satisfied with this problem.
Indeed, replication is performed by DNA polymerase,

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:52.970
but to initiate replication, DNA polymerase
needs a protein called DnaA.

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:59.970
DnaA binds to a short region (typically just
a 9 nucleotide-long segment) within the replication

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:06.240
origin that is known as a DnaA box.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:11.490
A DnaA box is actually the hidden message
that we are looking for, and the DnaA box

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:18.490
actually tells the DnaA protein: bind right
here! And the DnaA protein wants to see multiple

00:10:19.009 --> 00:10:25.360
DnaA boxes to bind efficiently.
So, it looks like we brought computational

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:32.019
people and biologists on the same page, and
we are now ready to try to solve this problem.

00:10:32.019 --> 00:10:38.529
Since it's the first problem at Rosalind,
we expect a wide variety of different algorithms

00:10:38.529 --> 00:10:43.610
proposed by you.
The simplest algorithm probably is this one.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:50.459
Start from the 1st k-mer in the Text, slide
it through the Text and see how many identical

00:10:50.459 --> 00:10:54.620
k-mers are present in the Text.
How much time it takes? It takes time roughly

00:10:54.620 --> 00:11:01.620
equal to |Text| ∙ k. But you need to do
it for every k-mer in Text, and there are

00:11:01.639 --> 00:11:05.889
roughly Text of them.
So, the overall running time of this very

00:11:05.889 --> 00:11:12.889
naïve algorithm is |Text2|∙ k.
If |Text| is one million, you may have problems,

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:18.029
but in our case |Text| is typically small.
The average length of the replication origin

00:11:18.029 --> 00:11:21.589
is just 500, maybe 700 hundred nucleotides.

00:11:21.589 --> 00:11:28.589
In this course, we will soon learn much more
sophisticated algorithms: for example, some

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:35.180
of you may be amazed that the same task can
be done incredibly fast. And we will cover
these algorithms in the class.

